## Response format

Return exactly one JSON object with these four keys and no others:

```json
{
  "hazard_edits": [
    {"hazard_id": "H-1", "description": "...", "related_losses": ["L-1"]}
  ],
  "hazard_additions": [
    {"handle": "new_hazard", "description": "...", "related_losses": ["L-1"]}
  ],
  "security_constraint_edits": [
    {"constraint_id": "SC-1", "rule": "...", "applies_when": [],
     "behavior_class": null,
     "obligations": [], "related_hazards": ["H-1"]}
  ],
  "security_constraint_additions": [
    {"handle": "new_constraint", "rule": "...", "applies_when": [],
     "behavior_class": "<class name or null>",
     "obligations": [], "related_hazards": ["new_hazard"]}
  ]
}
```

Use empty arrays when no edits or additions of a kind are needed. Preserve
every valid existing record by leaving it out of the delta.

## Worked examples

A check that a behavior class has no hazard of its own is fixed by adding
one new hazard with a `NEW-*` handle whose description names that class's
behavior and whose `related_losses` reference the supplied losses, then
covering that handle in the same response with a constraint addition or an
edit to an existing constraint's `related_hazards` (following the obligations
rules for edits).

The check reads each constraint's declared `behavior_class`, shown in the
constraint list. On an edit, `null` keeps the declared class and a class name
replaces it; change a class only when the declared one is wrong for the rule.

An edit that restates an existing record's content unchanged fixes nothing:
the merged graph is identical, the check re-fails, and the single revision
call is spent. When a failing check names a record pair, edit one of the
named records to repair that edge — never return a byte-identical edit or a
disconnected addition.

For every failing check, choose the smallest repair that changes the failing
edge in the merged graph:

- If an existing constraint points to the wrong hazard, edit that constraint's
  `related_hazards` to the correct supplied hazard ID. Keep its rule,
  applicability, and obligations unchanged unless the check also requires a
  wording repair. Do not add a second constraint while leaving the wrong edge.
- If a hazard is missing, add one local-handle hazard that references an
  existing supplied loss, then cover its handle in the same response with a
  constraint addition or an edit to an existing constraint's
  `related_hazards` (following the obligations rules for edits). If a
  constraint is missing, add one local-handle constraint that references an
  existing supplied hazard and states the supported rule. Never invent a loss,
  hazard subject, constraint meaning, relationship, or evidence merely to make
  a density check pass.

After mentally merging the delta, re-check every listed failure and the full
density gate on the complete graph, not only the named failure. A proposed
edit or addition that leaves any listed bad edge intact, creates a new
uncovered hazard, introduces an unresolved reference, or relies on unsupported
wording is not a repair and must not be returned.
