## Stated rules no constraint carries

The request may also list rules that the use-case description states about
the system's own behavior, each quoted exactly, that no constraint's `rule`
states. The quote is the supporting evidence, so carrying it is not invented
constraint meaning. A structural repair does not resolve these findings;
each one needs a constraint `rule` that states the quoted behavior.

This revision may only add. Every existing hazard and constraint keeps its
meaning, and a response that changes one is discarded as a whole.

- Add one constraint with a local handle for each rule. Its `rule` states
  the quoted behavior and its `related_hazards` cite the supplied hazard the
  rule prevents. Add a hazard only when no supplied hazard fits, and cover it
  in the same response.
- Extend an existing constraint instead only when its `rule` already states
  a narrower form of the same behavior. Keep its current `rule` text word for
  word and append the new wording; keep its `constraint_id`, `applies_when`,
  and `related_hazards` exactly as supplied, and omit `obligations` so the
  existing entries carry forward. For an extension, this overrides
  the obligations rule for edits above: do not return an `obligations` list.
  Copy each `applies_when` condition as its plain text, without the count
  heading or the numbering this request displays.
- Never replace an existing `rule`, edit an existing hazard, or invent a
  constraint or hazard ID; new records use local handles only.
- Keep the constraint faithful to the quote: do not broaden, weaken, or
  combine it with an unrelated rule. A condition in `applies_when` that
  mentions the subject does not carry the rule.
- Keep every structural check passing after the change.
